  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Vermont received $414M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 4,919 awards with an average award size of $84.2K. Obligations fell 52.8% year over year. FY2026 obligations are a partial fiscal-year total, so the FY26 figure is not a full-year total. Historical totals show year-to-year swings, including $394M in FY21, $506M in FY22, $424M in FY23, $920M in FY24, and $877M in FY25. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $251M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $62.2M and the Department of Homeland Security with $48.9M. GLOBALFOUNDRIES U.S. 2 LLC is the largest vendor with $87.5M in obligations. Semiconductor and Related Device Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $87.5M, and Engineering Services follows at $56.1M. This snapshot shows concentration in a few large buyers, a few large vendors, and select industries driving most federal spending in Vermont so far in FY2026.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in FY2026 compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 52.8% year over year. Annual totals show $920M in FY24 and $877M in FY25 before the FY26 partial total of $414M. Award activity remains sizable with 4,919 awards, but the average award size is $84.2K, reflecting the current mix of contract amounts. FY26 is a fiscal-year-to-date figure, not a full-year total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal buying in Vermont?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $251M, making it the largest buyer in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $62.2M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $48.9M. GLOBALFOUNDRIES U.S. 2 LLC received $87.5M, and SCIENCE APPLICATIONS INTERNATIONAL CORPORATION received $53.2M, showing vendor concentration among the top recipients."
      }
    ]
  },
